# Neurofibromatosis

- **Definition and terminology** - two clinically and genetically separated autosomal dominant conditions:
  - <u>Neurofibromatosis 1</u> - caused by mutation of NF1 gene of chromosome 17 (50% de novo) characterised by neurofibromas and cutaneous involvement
  - <u>Neurofibromatosis 2</u> - caused by mutation of NF2 gene of chromosome 22 characterised by schwanomas
- **Epidemiology** - NF1 is more common than NF2:
  - <u>Prevalence of NF1</u> - 20-50/100,000
  - <u>Prevalence of NF2</u> - 1.5/100,000
- **Clinical features of NF1 and NF2:** 
![](../img/clinical_features_of_nf1_and_nf2.png)
- **Malignant potential of neurofibromas and schwanommas:**
  - NF1 neurofibromas can undergo malignant change
  - NF2 schwanommas rarely undergo malignant change
